 "path": "tests/VObject/Recur/RRuleIteratorTest.php",
 "semantics": "sabre/vobject tests/VObject/Recur/RRuleIteratorTest.php, helper parse($rule, $start, $expected, $fastForward=null, $tz='UTC', $runTillTheEnd=false): new RRuleIterator($rule, new DateTime($start, new DateTimeZone($tz))); optional fastForward(new DateTime($fastForward)) (PHP default zone is UTC in the test bootstrap); then collects current()->format('Y-m-d H:i:s') while valid(); for infinite rules (no COUNT/UNTIL) it stops once as many values as expected were collected ('limit'), unless runTillTheEnd. RRuleIterator::rewind() sets the current date to DTSTART, so DTSTART is ALWAYS the first instance even when it does not match the rule, and it counts toward COUNT (include_dtstart=true, RFC 5545 semantics). Values are wall-clock times in $tz: tzid is set for non-UTC zones; for the default 'UTC' zone dtstart/expected carry a Z suffix. A date-only $start means midnight. fastForward(dt) skips instances < dt, so it is recorded as between.start (inclusive, no end; UTC because the PHP default zone is UTC). error=true: the test expects InvalidDataException. Several cases exercise DST gaps (Europe/Zurich, America/New_York): sabre keeps the nominal wall-clock sequence and moves non-existent local times forward by the gap; these expectations are sabre's behaviour where RFC 5545 is not explicit.",
